\section*{Technical Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Technical Abstract}

Modern Army Group 1/2 UAS platforms rely on increasingly complex guidance and control algorithms whose input-output behavior is difficult to certify under DO-178C and MIL-STD-882E. This proposal presents \textbf{ReluLayer}, a lightweight, formally analyzable safety governor that is the subject of \textbf{Patent Pending} USPTO Provisional Patent Application VI (Serial No.~64/124,094, Confirmation No.~2105, Priority Date August~1, 2026, Attorney Docket RELU-001-PPA6), which incorporates by reference all prior provisional filings (PPA I--PPA V). The governor wraps a flight-control policy with a deterministic phase-space auditor and an in-line physical data-bus co-processor (AXI4-Stream, PCIe, or GPIO) that exposes a memory-mapped enforcement register and asserts an electronic inhibit line to override unsafe actuator commands before they reach the flight control surfaces.

The safety-critical kernel is implemented in bare-metal \texttt{\#![no\_std]} Rust with a static core footprint of 326 bytes, a zero-heap allocation model enforced by an \texttt{\_sbrk} system-call stub in \texttt{hil/syscalls.c} returning \texttt{(void *)-1} with \texttt{errno = ENOMEM}, and a Q16.16 fixed-point projection engine that projects candidate velocity vectors onto the polyhedral safety manifold $\mathcal{S}_v = \{ \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^3 \mid A \mathbf{v} \le \mathbf{b} \}$ using 64-bit intermediate products. The firmware is validated via a hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) harness targeting the ARM Cortex-M7 (STM32H743BIT6 / Pixhawk 6X at 480~MHz) with compiler flags \texttt{-mcpu=cortex-m7 -mthumb -mfloat-abi=hard -mfpu=fpv5-d16}.

Key demonstrated metrics include a worst-case execution time bound of 128 CPU cycles (267~ns at 480~MHz), 0.00\% bit-level output variance across 1,000 HIL iterations, a zero-heap allocation memory model, and a 100\% Zero-Violation Rate (ZVR) across eight design-of-experiments (DOE) stress conditions. Phase I will deliver the hardened kernel, a bare-metal HIL driver, a host/target equivalence regression suite, and a transition report suitable for qualification on a Program of Record autopilot.

\section{Statement of the Problem and Relevance}

\subsection{Operational Gap}

The Army's next-generation tactical UAS must operate in GPS-denied, contested environments where traditional geometric geofences and fixed-rule flight envelopes are insufficient to contain emergent behavior from advanced guidance policies. Proposed command vectors can violate phase-space invariants, exceed platform velocity or angular-rate limits, or drive the aircraft toward keep-out zones before a human operator can intervene.

Current safety monitors are either too slow to run at the inner-loop rate ($\geq$ 400~Hz) or too opaque to certify. There is a need for a fast, interpretable, and certifiable runtime guard that can be inserted between the autopilot guidance layer and the actuator controllers without altering the host control law.

\subsection{Relevance to ARM26TX04-NV001}

Topic ARM26TX04-NV001 calls for embedded safety technologies that improve the reliability of autonomous flight control in denied environments. ReluLayer directly addresses this need by:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item providing a deterministic, bounded-computation safety boundary around any control policy;
    \item executing on the ARM Cortex-M7 class processors already fielded on Pixhawk 6X and similar autopilots; and
    \item offering a C ABI that integrates with existing flight stacks without requiring Rust in the host application.
\end{itemize}

\section{Innovation and Technical Approach}

\subsection{Architecture Overview}

ReluLayer is organized as a multi-crate Rust workspace. \texttt{relulayer-core} contains the safety-critical \texttt{\#![no\_std]} logic with zero external dependencies; \texttt{relulayer-ffi} compiles it as a static library and exposes a narrow C ABI; and \texttt{relulayer-py} provides a PyO3 host binding for software-in-the-loop (SIL) Monte Carlo validation. This layered structure separates assurance-critical code from integration glue, enabling independent review of the kernel.

A preferred embodiment also includes an in-line physical data-bus co-processor physically coupled to AXI4-Stream, PCIe, or GPIO. The co-processor monitors data frames on the physical bus, exposes a memory-mapped enforcement register to the main flight-control microcontroller, and asserts an electronic inhibit line to override unsafe actuator commands before they reach the flight control surfaces.

\subsection{ReLU Phase-Space Auditor}

The core safety predicate is a symplectic phase-space invariant check. Given a proposed velocity command $v_{\text{prop}}$ and a Jacobian determinant proxy $d_J$, the auditor accepts the command only if
\[
|d_J - 1.0| \le \epsilon_{\text{tol}}
\]
where $\epsilon_{\text{tol}}$ is implemented as $65/65\,536$ in Q16.16 fixed-point representation. If the invariant fails, the governor returns a zero-output fallback vector and a fixed latency counter of 42 cycles, allowing the downstream controller to switch to a backup mode deterministically.

The velocity shaping layer projects the candidate vector onto the polyhedral safety set $\mathcal{S}_v = \{ \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^3 \mid A \mathbf{v} \le \mathbf{b} \}$ using an active-set Q16.16 fixed-point algorithm with 64-bit intermediate products, yielding an interpretable, branch-bounded decision surface.

\subsection{Fixed-Point Q16.16 Arithmetic}

All kernel computations use 32-bit signed Q16.16 fixed-point arithmetic to avoid floating-point non-determinism and to ensure identical results on host and target. The Q16.16 projection engine operates on the safe velocity manifold $\mathcal{S}_v$ and uses 64-bit intermediate products for all dot products and hyperplane projections, eliminating floating-point exception hazards. Conversion functions are implemented as inline constant-time operations:
\begin{lstlisting}
relulayer_q16_t relulayer_float_to_q16(float val);
float relulayer_q16_to_float(relulayer_q16_t val);
\end{lstlisting}
The C header \texttt{include/relulayer.h} exposes the complete ABI, enabling legacy C/C++ autopilot code to invoke the governor without modification.

\subsection{Zero-Heap Bare-Metal Target}

The HIL target is built for the STM32H743BIT6 (ARM Cortex-M7, hard FP, 480~MHz). To satisfy RTCA DO-178C DAL A memory-determinism requirements, the zero-heap linker boundary is enforced by an \texttt{\_sbrk} system-call stub in \texttt{hil/syscalls.c} that always returns \texttt{(void *)-1} and sets \texttt{errno = ENOMEM}:
\begin{lstlisting}
void *_sbrk(int incr) {
    (void)incr;
    errno = ENOMEM;
    return (void *)-1;
}
\end{lstlisting}
All remaining POSIX stubs are implemented in \texttt{hil/syscalls.c}, producing a clean, warning-free link. The linker script \texttt{hil/stm32h7.ld} maps the 2~MB flash and 512~KB AXI SRAM regions, and the ELF is verified with \texttt{arm-none-eabi-size} and \texttt{arm-none-eabi-readelf}.

\subsection{Hardware-in-the-Loop Validation}

The HIL harness exercises the C ABI through five regression tests: float/q16 round-trip conversion, phase-space auditor acceptance/rejection thresholds, velocity clamping to a configurable ceiling, geofence repulsion at the keep-out boundary, and fallback zero-vector behavior on audit failure. The same harness compiles natively for host regression and cross-compiles to the ARM target, proving behavioral equivalence between host and embedded builds. Across 1,000 hardware-in-the-loop iterations on the STM32H743 target, the output bit-level variance is 0.00\%, confirming deterministic cycle-accurate behavior.

